  "body": "## Summary\n\nReplaces v2.1.2's hardcoded 5 s recovery scan with a poll-until-`org.bluez.Device1`-is-populated loop.  Fixes the *\"Pairing failed: interface not found on this object: org.bluez.Device1\"* error on Start when the relay has been idle long enough (>~30 s) for BlueZ to strip the `Device1` interface from a previously-connected device.\n\n## The bug\n\nUser report on `larson-base.lan`: Start the relay after >5 min idle → fails with:\n\n```\nFailed to prepare Bluetooth device: Device setup failed after recovery scan:\n  Pairing failed: interface not found on this object: org.bluez.Device1\n```\n\nWorkaround that worked: manually click **Scan for Devices** in the sp-rtk-base UI (20 s scan), then Start.  Sometimes required **two** sequential scans, not one.\n\n## Root cause\n\nBlueZ has a **two-phase rediscovery** pattern on a stale device path:\n\n| Phase | Trigger | `org.bluez.Device1` attached? |\n|-------|---------|-------------------------------|\n| 1 | First short scan after eviction | ❌ stub only |\n| 2 | ~20-30 s of active discovery, OR a second scan, OR a successful RFCOMM | ✅ |\n\nv2.1.2's 5 s recovery scan only got to phase 1 — the path existed, but the `Device1` interface wasn't there yet, so the immediate retry of `_pair_and_trust` hit the same `interface not found` error and gave up.\n\n## Fix — `bluetooth_manager.py`\n\nNew helper `_async_wait_for_device_interface(mac, scan_timeout)`:\n\n- Loop at 2 s intervals.\n- Invalidate the introspection cache → re-introspect the device path → check whether the returned Node's `interfaces` list contains `org.bluez.Device1`.\n- If absent, ensure `Adapter1.StartDiscovery` is running (idempotent — \"InProgress\" treated as success), sleep, retry.\n- Returns as soon as `Device1` appears.  Zero overhead when the device is already known to BlueZ (the cache hits on the first introspect and the loop exits).\n- Raises `BluetoothError` with an actionable message after `scan_timeout` seconds.\n\n`ensure_device_ready` now calls this helper **before** `_pair_and_trust` and drops the old retry-with-recovery-scan block (now redundant).\n\n## Tuning\n\n- `BluetoothConfig.scan_timeout` default: **10 → 30**.  Empirically reliable on a consistently-advertising device.  Zero-overhead for warm-cache cases.\n- `BluetoothInputSource.connect` propagates `self.config.scan_timeout` to `ensure_device_ready` so per-deployment tuning works end-to-end.\n\n## Test changes\n\n| Test | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `mock_bluetooth.MockMessageBus.introspect` | Returns a `MockNode` with path-specific interfaces (was: XML string) so the poll loop can inspect `node.interfaces` |\n| `test_ensure_device_ready_retries_after_failure` → `..._waits_for_interface_then_succeeds` | Patches the poll to register the device mid-loop |\n| `test_ensure_device_ready_fails_after_retry` → `..._fails_when_interface_never_appears` | Pins the new error text + asserts MAC + actionable hint |\n| `test_config_defaults` | Asserts `scan_timeout=30` |\n| `test_connect_success_with_*` | Asserts `scan_timeout=30` propagates to `ensure_device_ready` |\n\n## Verification\n\n- **1145 unit tests pass** (was 1138).\n- `ruff check` + format clean.\n- `mypy --strict` + `pyright --strict`: 0 errors each.\n\n## Next step\n\n`sp-rtk-base` v0.3.13 will bump its constraint to `sp_rtk_base_relay>=2.1.3` to pick up this fix.  Without that bump, the deployed `sp-rtk-base` keeps using `relay==2.1.2` and the bug stays."
